Architecture plan

Scope reviewed
- Issue #3: Build the owl as inline SVG.
- Issue #4: Page layout and responsive styling.
- Issue #5: End-to-end smoke test and cross-viewport check.
- PRODUCT.md defines this as a minimal static web page skeleton with plain HTML and CSS, no framework, no build pipeline, no JavaScript, backend, or database.

Module structure and file organization
- Keep the project as a root-level static site.
- index.html:
  - Replace the placeholder content with the final semantic page structure.
  - Inline the owl SVG directly in the main content area.
  - Group SVG primitives with labeled parts for body, eyes, beak, wings, and feet.
  - Preserve document metadata, viewport metadata, and linked stylesheet.
- styles.css:
  - Own all page-level presentation: viewport layout, background, typography, responsive scaling, SVG sizing, and caption/title spacing.
  - Use CSS custom properties only if they clarify repeated colors or sizing.
- tests/ or e2e/:
  - Add Playwright smoke coverage only when issue #5 is implemented.
  - Keep test files separate from static page source.
- package.json and Playwright config:
  - Add only when issue #5 needs a runnable browser test command.

Shared types and modules
- None for issues #3 and #4. The app remains plain HTML/CSS.
- Issue #5 may introduce test helper selectors, but no runtime application types are needed.

Dependencies between issues
- Issue #3 should be implemented first because the page layout and smoke test need a real owl SVG target.
- Issue #4 depends on the SVG structure from issue #3 so responsive sizing can be applied to stable elements and labels.
- Issue #5 depends on issues #3 and #4 because it should validate the final rendered SVG and completed responsive layout at mobile, tablet, and desktop widths.

Key design decisions
- Continue with plain HTML and CSS until a future issue explicitly requires more tooling.
- Use inline SVG instead of image files so the owl remains inspectable, accessible, and directly testable.
- Give the SVG a stable accessible name and stable selectors or data attributes suitable for Playwright assertions.
- Keep the visual system modest and static-site friendly: centered composition, responsive SVG sizing, and no decorative dependencies.
- Introduce Playwright only for end-to-end testing, not for application runtime behavior.
